course: english
lesson: 14
title: Words to know, Years 5–6 (the statutory list, by idea)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Years 5–6 list in England has about 100 harder words: accommodate, ancient, conscience, desperate, embarrass, necessary, privilege, rhythm, sincerely, sufficient, temperature and more. Many come from Latin, Greek or French, and grown-ups misspell them too. The way in is the same as before: find the tricky bit, group words that share it, and give it a story.
explain: Double letters are the classic trap. «Accommodate» has double c and double m, room for everyone. «Embarrass» has double r and double s: embarrassed people go Really Red And Smile Shyly. «Necessary» has one collar and two sleeves: one c, two s's. «Privilege» has no d at all, though people often add one.
explain: Some words hide letters that fast speech drops. «Desperate» is des-per-ate, not «desprate»; «temperature» is tem-per-a-ture, not «temprature». Saying the word slowly, in parts, keeps every letter. Other words share an odd pattern: «ancient», «conscience» and «conscious» all spell a /sh/ sound with ci or sci; «rhyme» and «rhythm» both start with rh («rhythm» comes from Greek, and «rhyme» borrowed its rh from it about 400 years ago).
explain: In British English some pairs split by job. Advice and practice (with c) are nouns, things; advise and practise (with s) are verbs, doing words: «Take my advice; I advise you to practise». A trick: ice is a noun. American English uses «practice» for both. Also British «programme» means a TV show or a plan, while a computer «program» is spelled the same in both countries.
explain: These words live in letters and formal writing. A polite letter ending «Yours sincerely» that suggests a sufficient number of chairs uses three list words at once, so writing a short letter is great practice for spelling and style together.
fact: The Years 5–6 statutory list in England has about 100 words, many from Latin, Greek or French.
fact: «Accommodate» has double c and double m.
fact: «Embarrass» has double r and double s.
fact: «Necessary» has one c and two s's: one collar, two sleeves.
fact: «Privilege» has no d.
fact: «Desperate» is des-per-ate and «temperature» is tem-per-a-ture; fast speech often drops a part.
fact: «Ancient», «conscience» and «conscious» spell a /sh/ sound with ci or sci.
fact: «Rhyme» and «rhythm» both start with rh; «rhythm» is from Greek and «rhyme» took its rh from «rhythm».
fact: In British English «advice» and «practice» are nouns, «advise» and «practise» are verbs; American English uses «practice» for both.
fact: In British English a TV show or plan is a «programme»; a computer «program» is spelled the same in Britain and America.
fact: Many list words, such as «sincerely», «suggest» and «sufficient», are common in letters and formal writing.
fact: Slang in this lesson: «nesh» means feeling the cold easily and «cuppa» means a cup of tea (Britain); «dreich» is Scottish for dreary and wet; «mad» before a word means very, as in «mad hard».
exercise: Hana says «accommodate» has double letters everywhere. Which ones? | double c and double m | double c and double d | double m and double t | double o and double a
exercise: Jake asks why «ancient», «conscience» and «conscious» are learned together. What do they share? | A /sh/ sound spelled with ci or sci | They all have a silent k | They all end in -ous | They are all words about history
exercise: The Leeds mum says «desperate, love, not desprate». Which part is often dropped in speech? | the e after the p | the d at the start | the t at the very end | the s in the middle
exercise: What is a «cuppa»? | A cup of tea | A small cupboard | A football cup | A small cupcake
exercise: Oliver writes «embarass». What is right? | embarrass | embarras | embbarrass | embarrasse
exercise: What do «rhyme» and «rhythm» have in common? | Both start with rh | Both end in -m | Both have two y's | Both are Latin words about music and poems
exercise: Ade asks if «programme» is the same as «program». In British English, which is a TV show? | programme | program | programm | prograam
exercise: Max writes «priviledge». What is right? | privilege | privelege | privilage | priviledge
exercise: What does «mad hard» mean? | Very hard | Angry and hard | A little hard | Hard but fun
exercise: Isla says the weather is «dreich». What is it like? | Dreary and wet | Hot and very sunny | Snowy and bright | Windy but quite dry
exercise: Isla is learning «temperature». How many syllables does it have when said slowly? | 4: tem-per-a-ture | 3: tem-pra-ture | 2: temp-ture | 5: te-mp-er-a-ture
exercise: Which pair is noun, then verb, in British English? | advice, advise | advise, advice | practise, practice | advise, practise
exercise: Jasmine writes «neccessary». What is right? | necessary | neccesary | necesary | neccessary
exercise: Which trick helps with «necessary»? | One collar, two sleeves | Two collars, one sleeve | Never eat cake | Ness is sorry
exercise: Why practise list words in a letter? | Letters use many of these formal words | Letters are shorter than tests | Teachers only read letters | Letters don't need spelling
exercise: Which spelling is right? | sufficient | sufficiant | suficient | sufficeint
test: «I'll take your advise, then I'll practice». Which British spellings are right? | advice (noun), practise (verb) | advise (noun), practice (verb) | advice (noun), practice (verb) | advise (noun), practise (verb)
test: «accomodate, one m, sorted, it's mad hard anyway». What's the slip? | It has double m as well as double c | Nothing, «accomodate» is right | It needs a double d | It needs only one c too
test: «tempratur, that's how I say it, it's dreich and I'm done». What would help? | Say it slowly in parts: tem-per-a-ture | Write it the way you say it | Use a shorter word, like «heat», forever | Learn it by its shape only
test: Ade's teacher in London writes «computer program». Ade says it should be «programme». Who's right? | The teacher: a computer program is «program» everywhere | Ade: always «programme» in Britain | Both are wrong: it is «programm» | Ade: «program» is American only
test: What would happen if you only practised list words in a spelling test, never in writing? | You might spell them in tests but not use them well in writing | You'd use them perfectly in every letter | You would forget how to read them | Nothing, tests and writing are the same
test: «embarassed has one r, bruv». Which trick fixes it? | Really Red And Smile Shyly: double r and double s | Every big word has only one r, so one r is enough here too | Embarrassed rhymes with «barest» | Write it as it sounds: imbarist
test: In a cartoon, a sign says «Neccessary items only». What is wrong? | One c, not two: «necessary» | Two s's should be one: «necesary» | It should be spelled «nessessary» | Nothing; that's the right spelling
test: «rhyme comes from Greek like rhythm, obvs». What's more accurate? | «Rhythm» is Greek; «rhyme» took its rh from «rhythm» | Both are Latin words | Neither has rh in it | «Rhyme» is Greek, «rhythm» is French
test: «ancient? that's a sh sound, so ansh-ent». How is the /sh/ spelled? | ci, as in «ancient» | sh, as in «anshent» | ti, as in «antient» | ch, as in «anchent»
test: ★★★ How many words are spelled correctly: privilege, embarrass, necessary, desprate, accommodate? | 4 | 5 | 3 | 2
test: ★★★ In British English, which sentence is spelled correctly? | I need more practice, so I'll practise tonight | I need more practise, so I'll practice tonight | I need more practise, so I'll practise tonight | I need more practice, so I'll practice tonight
test: ★★★ Which word has the SAME number of double letters as «accommodate»? | embarrass | necessary | privilege | desperate
exam: Ascertain the correct spelling of the word signifying «required» or «indispensable». | necessary | neccessary | neccesary | necesary
exam: Identify the principal distinction, in British usage, between «advice» and «advise». | «Advice» is a noun, whereas «advise» is a verb | «Advice» is a verb, whereas «advise» is a noun | «Advise» is the American form of «advice» | The two forms are interchangeable in all contexts
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the words «programme» and «program» in British English. | «Programme» denotes a broadcast or plan, whereas «program» denotes computer software | «Programme» is obsolete and has been replaced by «program» | «Program» denotes a broadcast, whereas «programme» denotes software | Both forms are incorrect in British English, the correct form being «programm» in all its senses
exam: Which of the following methods most accurately assists a pupil in the spelling of words frequently shortened in speech, such as «temperature»? | The pronunciation of the word slowly, syllable by syllable | The spelling of the word as it is spoken rapidly | The substitution of a shorter word in all writing | The memorisation of the word by its shape
